WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:03.029
Hi everyone, in this lesson,

2
00:00:03.029 --> 00:00:06.309
we're gonna do a brief overview of the Megascans tool

3
00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:08.189
to be able to make layered textures

4
00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.929
and some things to look out for.

5
00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:11.810
So let's get started.

6
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.890
So to be able to make a blended material

7
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.210
using Megascans, you have to keep some things in mind.

8
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.289
One of them, you gotta make sure, of course,

9
00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:21.730
that you do need to be logged into Quixel Bridge

10
00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:22.949
and have the latest,

11
00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:24.969
but also when you're working with your materials

12
00:00:24.969 --> 00:00:27.089
to make sure the materials are the latest

13
00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:28.929
or the newest type materials.

14
00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:32.170
Now, the reason why, if I chose an older material

15
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.070
and to say, I've already downloaded it

16
00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:35.929
and maybe it's inside of my system,

17
00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:37.890
like, oh, you know, Megascans,

18
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.729
what do we have?

19
00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:40.909
Megascans wise, I don't think I have any Megascans

20
00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:42.689
in this particular example.

21
00:00:42.689 --> 00:00:44.130
Oh, there we go.

22
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.890
And we have Megascans, we'll go to our surfaces here

23
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.009
and say, I got a coal mine.

24
00:00:49.009 --> 00:00:52.689
If it is a older material

25
00:00:52.689 --> 00:00:55.649
and it's paired with an also an older material,

26
00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:58.549
these are both kind of old, the Clover ideal,

27
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.429
it would actually work pretty well.

28
00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:03.869
But if I decided to go to a brand new released material,

29
00:01:03.869 --> 00:01:06.849
say this one, it would be very difficult

30
00:01:06.849 --> 00:01:10.549
to be able to get these and download them

31
00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:12.549
and have them in my scene.

32
00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:14.870
And then once they're selected,

33
00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.510
get them into the blending

34
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.189
because the way they're structured from old to new

35
00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:21.390
is very different.

36
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.450
The newer versions are a lot more efficient

37
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.019
in being able to customize the material.

38
00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:30.579
The older ones, not quite as much.

39
00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:32.480
And when you try to pair them together,

40
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.420
Quixel Bridge can get confused

41
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.900
and you'll see it here in the interface.

42
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.019
There's a little kind of like a bar

43
00:01:41.019 --> 00:01:43.099
to be able to change values in here.

44
00:01:43.099 --> 00:01:46.260
This used to be able to be a different setting

45
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.519
where you can actually control some more details

46
00:01:48.519 --> 00:01:49.939
about what you're getting.

47
00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.540
Now it kind of is turned into the button

48
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.980
that takes us to how to blend

49
00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.500
and maybe switch a 3D master material and so forth.

50
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.219
So if you wanted to,

51
00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.780
you would want to select a couple materials

52
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:03.939
that you would need

53
00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:06.260
and you would say create a material blend

54
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.180
and you can do that through your content browser.

55
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.259
So I could choose maybe the clover here,

56
00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:13.259
click on that interface again and say,

57
00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:18.259
hey man, I'm gonna create a material that I want to blend.

58
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.539
Now you got to make sure that they are selected.

59
00:02:21.539 --> 00:02:23.340
So I would have to like kind of make sure

60
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.780
they're in the same kind of folder or something.

61
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.620
That's actually nice

62
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:29.139
because then you'll know which ones you've blended

63
00:02:29.139 --> 00:02:31.280
and you would select them at the same time

64
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.060
and then you would make your blended material.

65
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.699
So they need to be selected in your content browser,

66
00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:36.939
clicking this interface

67
00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:40.259
and then you will say collect, create material blend

68
00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:43.139
and it'll put it, it will call it material blend

69
00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:45.020
and it'll put it where you want to put it.

70
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.500
So you can create a folder for it

71
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.020
which should work just fine.

72
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.219
Now again, the caveat is just a reminder,

73
00:02:52.219 --> 00:02:54.979
if you have a brand new, new surface material

74
00:02:54.979 --> 00:02:56.819
like in this new surface category

75
00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:00.780
and I have something old like this grass and clover leaf

76
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.139
that may not match up.

77
00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:04.219
So when you try to blend them, you may get an error

78
00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:07.000
and may just say it did it, but it's just blank.

79
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.379
I've had that, doesn't quite work

80
00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:11.460
and I personally don't prefer this method

81
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.460
and I'll show you why

82
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.460
cause it really just does a fade

83
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.580
which can easily be done the old school way

84
00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.939
or the new school way

85
00:03:17.939 --> 00:03:21.580
and you don't necessarily need quick soul bridge

86
00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:22.699
to do it for you.

87
00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:23.719
So let me show you what I mean.

88
00:03:23.719 --> 00:03:25.259
So I'm gonna click on this particular material.

89
00:03:25.259 --> 00:03:27.340
I'm gonna double click on it, open it up

90
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.979
and you'll see that we have our wood

91
00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:34.520
and clover already kind of blended together

92
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.219
but it's blended in such a way

93
00:03:36.219 --> 00:03:38.740
that it just kind of fades into each other.

94
00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:43.439
So I'll see that I have a layer base albedo color

95
00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:45.159
that I can control and a few other things

96
00:03:45.159 --> 00:03:48.219
but we also have base layer rotation angle

97
00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:51.219
and normal strength so I can push normals through.

98
00:03:52.219 --> 00:03:55.060
And you'll see here, even with the normals

99
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.020
it's really just fading into each other is all it's doing.

100
00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.740
We can do the roughness here

101
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.000
if we wanted to pull in the roughness

102
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.539
from one to the other.

103
00:04:04.539 --> 00:04:07.419
And it's really again, stacked in such a way

104
00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:09.900
so that it's simply just fading into each other.

105
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.289
There's the UV control

106
00:04:12.289 --> 00:04:15.150
and you can see that we can move one away

107
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.050
then the other middle layer normal strength

108
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.360
get that UV control back to zero

109
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:25.220
and that's really all it does.

110
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.560
It's nothing necessarily to write home about.

111
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.319
It's nothing you can't do on your own if you wanted to.

112
00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:33.819
So it's really up to you how you build it.

113
00:04:33.819 --> 00:04:35.920
I don't necessarily prefer this way.

114
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.879
We can even turn on the top layer here

115
00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.399
but this is really how you wanna do it.

116
00:04:41.399 --> 00:04:43.240
Now I don't have more than just one.

117
00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.839
You can choose more than just one or two

118
00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:47.680
and even add a puddle layer if you wanted to.

119
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.740
There's no spec for it to reach out for that in here

120
00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.379
that's detailed in that way

121
00:04:53.379 --> 00:04:56.420
but that's kind of how this system works.

122
00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.180
Again, it's nothing you can't do

123
00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.220
with the old system and the new system.

124
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.379
So I don't necessarily recommend it because again

125
00:05:03.379 --> 00:05:05.540
it's easy to forget your old material

126
00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.600
versus your new material

127
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.480
and you could run into that major hurdle.

128
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.139
All right, so that's it with this video.

129
00:05:11.139 --> 00:05:12.199
I just wanted to point that out

130
00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:15.339
that that option is there and some of those caveats.

131
00:05:15.339 --> 00:05:17.420
In the next video, we're gonna start breaking down

132
00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.180
how to create this type of layered material

133
00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.199
which is our new way of being able to control

134
00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:24.740
and create layered materials.

135
00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:25.579
Thanks again.
